    {
      "type": "examAlert",
      "items": [
        "The Deccan Traps of Maharashtra are frequently cited in exams as a classic example of Flood Basalt Province volcanism.",
        "Questions often contrast Shield Volcanoes (basaltic, low viscosity) with Composite Volcanoes (viscous lava, pyroclastic layers).",
        "Calderas are distinguished in MCQs as collapsed volcanic depressions formed by the most violent eruptions."
      ],
      "id": "kqi2oy1"
    },
    {
      "type": "keyFacts",
      "title": "Key Takeaways",
      "items": [
        "Shield volcanoes are predominantly composed of basaltic lava, creating gentle slopes and exhibiting low explosivity unless water enters the vent.",
        "Composite volcanoes are characterized by eruptions of cooler, more viscous lava along with pyroclastic material and ash.",
        "Calderas are the most explosive volcanic structures, often collapsing inward during an eruption to form deep depressions.",
        "Flood Basalt Provinces release huge volumes of highly fluid lava that spread across thousands of square kilometers, such as India's Deccan Traps.",
        "Mid-Ocean Ridge Volcanoes occur underwater along a global ocean ridge network spanning more than 70,000 kilometers."
      ],
      "id": "zozwhg6"
    }
